Since ![](images/de/def1e6afa80a61af78e69da17bdf65200185f760454a98ffa8b56142cd99b08f.svg) as ![](images/3a/3a4f4cfc9409e86d8918647d161f6cccccd1ba270e603575b8fc9f5167e58a48.svg) these solutions approach the origin as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg)\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/ff/ff535f17581c5a93045c3c23b8b33ca2996be6dfca63ed7808bc5fec809d2377.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/d5/d57858ae72e3b836de8c930917b65bcfef13cd3b85518933523024f84905a05b.svg) has the equation \n![](images/8f/8f96aa73ce843c7815a1fed6bf3f3774a173cfa4d56f13e1f3a8c48d53cd7097.svg)\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/b2/b2a991e2b459a17fc02d58d3eb267f04c03d03f8224561bc234513aa5dfc8a7a.svg)\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ![](images/b2/b2e3de236be1367c614d785fd065d91340a14b9197fb77734c537161645f69ba.svg) as ![](images/3a/3a4f4cfc9409e86d8918647d161f6cccccd1ba270e603575b8fc9f5167e58a48.svg) these solutions approach the origin as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg)\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/31/317fe507f591931ea371db43b78a83b1741c427ef89dd90571f0a0030ed70c20.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n![](images/87/878b11e2e3f3f3aaa6133482d483f1aa22ff420ade2a3d9533692f04c47348d1.svg)\nthus, ![](images/e2/e2c67900a3ba263aad1f200c892be4d755ab441e6b96ad6317e9364121f597b4.svg) is the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue).\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is ![](images/41/413cdb74711dd235ff727690ee5fb8713e6f50cbb49d131121a40b18b0943f53.svg) solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ![](images/ed/ed99403d5ac94993d00ca1f9c99def2e8d3bcbddba68d8aa1c1a8651fd140e2a.svg) as they move towards the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/3a/3a6d8a97c6d07c78562c73dbd1abef1b1a3c0d9f38cd14f4f9874df091492b9e.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166917 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a8dac9e3-6d64-5716-8812-6c8cce0790e7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166544 :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n![](images/0c/0c4b7d443c58e6720389306e73eb4e5e1934cfb7359221f36fb43ceb3e91e501.svg)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/32/3244f8128366a3f7f4ae3e41cd9323b1712f9587943f9fb2de54ee0c458feedf.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/d2/d241239c6475fbcf9559c2fcbbb8ca2805a6692fd1a9b3966fe8b38441075a4d.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](images/c3/c3ac6997c0e8580f8d91b88578a02043ca495e99e3ea67bdf91b3eab9eb43d98.svg)\nmust approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_1 >  0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/77/77200c9fefd57e0b82b796072e07411f5fe10c91185d7d8f665f7ef6c3f54360.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= 0,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/fb/fb40117d63e76b2b2db59313b173b04a9a23ef2a28493a5041584e4c7c60c67d.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](images/0c/0ce3220f2cd013daba6b1f44f88d2834021b44ba877980f5ec47df7bc52396ce.svg)\nmust approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_2 > 0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are positive. The dominant eigenvalue is the one with the greatest magnitude.\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is $\\lambda_2.$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda_2 > \\lambda_1 > 0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166918 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5c8c49ce-183e-525d-b069-5b56d43e69f7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166543 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n![](images/9b/9b19dee8e197c29ab1802c7ea500f07185f24885702027a9726de39a91eacd9e.svg)\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/15/150fe59c158191129d89c43a1bb569e5c88b686c4365a6b06e661343f85b5e7d.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -\\dfrac12x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/f6/f6679165a59f48eb2944f4f8337ba990f2e82619e5e2a03308e0b4f663f9137c.svg)\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, $y=-\\dfrac12x$ is an unstable line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/e1/e1dcfabda7582534138b11b45a14cd8a8a9923c02d743ff99af08e487bcda254.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/3b/3ba48e81a2f6239f7cf0389017d823b87d558ded003eb6470ff6dc9078808b87.svg)\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, $y=-2x$ is a stable line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is a saddle point. \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/27/2710e11309547500aa3e9ba89f31281cbbc28244509046ed25a6c151c10c23ed.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-21379b36-910e-5f85-8d70-2437c1f661cd" :question/id #uuid "21379b36-910e-5f85-8d70-2437c1f661cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-332918" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }-2]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/61/617f0b123b98086ed902041c1a601d44ac264ab97acfc9e2af25ca330699d98f.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6e/6e410f51824c79ee39d8f514b72926ce861108d39df4c1ac58bbb9c2d3210eae.png)\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/d3/d3449435602d6b160490462ab97545bb2aba0cc7fce498de2df8a226b90e0a49.png)" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\n Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach  $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/61/617f0b123b98086ed902041c1a601d44ac264ab97acfc9e2af25ca330699d98f.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\n Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach  $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6e/6e410f51824c79ee39d8f514b72926ce861108d39df4c1ac58bbb9c2d3210eae.png)\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/d3/d3449435602d6b160490462ab97545bb2aba0cc7fce498de2df8a226b90e0a49.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0a61b79e-688c-5e6c-90f2-7a326364b29a" "answer-field-3e7c5409-eb36-557f-bde7-075b3e94d6b8" "answer-field-f9f1c5cb-9a71-5305-8195-bf7acc2a7402" "answer-field-4473a54d-9b0c-5187-a682-a1e613818091" "answer-field-15fc3c0c-ec08-5611-8ecc-38062ebef547" "answer-field-7f65ef7a-c37d-50df-84e6-29896149422f" "answer-field-c3594b73-6f5e-59d7-af33-eca899d7a69b" "answer-field-2d2a05c9-940f-5781-bdab-ff4b586605b4" "answer-field-92a7fa11-5d50-5798-8884-d2bbd08a949a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0a61b79e-688c-5e6c-90f2-7a326364b29a" :answer-field/id #uuid "0a61b79e-688c-5e6c-90f2-7a326364b29a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-2d796dae-20eb-5f89-acd8-23fb3cd413f0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8a060391-3418-5f02-b3b2-645424856975" "answer-2d796dae-20eb-5f89-acd8-23fb3cd413f0" "answer-e61ec0ec-6c90-5c77-8361-ca671ce4fedd" "answer-55e6ae08-689f-5fd2-bac3-b15d7629b2ce"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a060391-3418-5f02-b3b2-645424856975" :answer/id #uuid "8a060391