nswer-field-2d2a05c9-940f-5781-bdab-ff4b586605b4" :answer-field/id #uuid "2d2a05c9-940f-5781-bdab-ff4b586605b4" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-54d39502-03b2-5d2d-a61c-c73c6fff90d4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-54d39502-03b2-5d2d-a61c-c73c6fff90d4" "answer-e0129d82-8c86-5401-8edc-9859f2dccc4d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54d39502-03b2-5d2d-a61c-c73c6fff90d4" :answer/id #uuid "54d39502-03b2-5d2d-a61c-c73c6fff90d4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "infinity" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0129d82-8c86-5401-8edc-9859f2dccc4d" :answer/id #uuid "e0129d82-8c86-5401-8edc-9859f2dccc4d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the origin" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-92a7fa11-5d50-5798-8884-d2bbd08a949a" :answer-field/id #uuid "92a7fa11-5d50-5798-8884-d2bbd08a949a" :answer-field/key "field-9" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-d4ece596-246a-5a2c-a0d4-ad11b295364d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d4ece596-246a-5a2c-a0d4-ad11b295364d" "answer-91fd4191-bff0-5ebf-9f2d-af16b20fb315"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4ece596-246a-5a2c-a0d4-ad11b295364d" :answer/id #uuid "d4ece596-246a-5a2c-a0d4-ad11b295364d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "phase portrait" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91fd4191-bff0-5ebf-9f2d-af16b20fb315" :answer/id #uuid "91fd4191-bff0-5ebf-9f2d-af16b20fb315" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "vector field" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-16c55b82-84fe-59ee-bb9a-f6d748d9f231" :question/id #uuid "16c55b82-84fe-59ee-bb9a-f6d748d9f231" :question/math-academy-id "q-333025" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{5t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/2a/2ab8c69672acf64e93615960fdbb34445d4f81617cae16f90a8e4f30252607ba.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/d1/d1b7ffd22ad77fe94a994659fb7a091692b23bafb3012a705e964419fb71e8aa.png)\n\nFinally, since the dominant eigenvalue is \n{{field-9}},\nsolutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector\n{{field-10}} \nas they move away from the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7a/7a87773278ef8c921a9e7002a376bac1de99e969c2eb10e4a6f104a854094469.png)" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/2a/2ab8c69672acf64e93615960fdbb34445d4f81617cae16f90a8e4f30252607ba.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=0\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/d1/d1b7ffd22ad77fe94a994659fb7a091692b23bafb3012a705e964419fb71e8aa.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n\n$$\n0<{\\lambda }_{2}<{\\lambda }_{1}\n$$\n\nthus, ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ is the dominant eigenvalue.\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{1}$ as they move away from the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7a/7a87773278ef8c921a9e7002a376bac1de99e969c2eb10e4a6f104a854094469.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-06bc73cd-c613-5fa8-8f61-271a439487d5" "answer-field-2030e323-2830-549b-a1b1-97f57ea09ba3" "answer-field-1cac7a69-9a04-56da-90af-dafaa1efa6ce" "answer-field-7aae5d2f-f06a-5c24-b202-bfcc9244da01" "answer-field-7fbadde8-a887-5982-a0c4-352802ef2291" "answer-field-314de6cd-4