26252a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308361 :question/problem "![](images/56/56a90f9fb61640cc2eeca8e2f21f69381a1d53d008701ec457ade0fed7e54a64.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. \nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307544 :answer-field/correct 17592186305654 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307885 :answer-field/correct 17592186306059 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307838 :answer-field/correct 17592186306298 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307692 :answer-field/correct 17592186307300 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307830 :answer-field/correct 17592186305221 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307915 :answer-field/correct 17592186306443 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-5c8c49ce-183e-525d-b069-5b56d43e69f7" :question/id #uuid "5c8c49ce-183e-525d-b069-5b56d43e69f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-333625" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }3]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-1}} is {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\n$y=-3x$ is {{field-3}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-3x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-3x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f2/f20c6478af528b45ab2ea7dfded3e835c49a79178e11d7dd06bb13760874bbdb.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-17f61bfc-2b2f-5780-a0d3-c92c029fb198" "answer-field-2b80139d-bc1e-5645-8de4-30d2db8afdf1" "answer-field-4ad00cd6-77d9-5b4b-9995-246f45c8c4b3" "answer-field-80de76c1-383f-57a7-b028-3b3844fe8b00"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-17f61bfc-2b2f-5780-a0d3-c92c029fb198" :answer-field/id #uuid "17f61bfc-2b2f-5780-a0d3-c92c029fb198" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-cffa05d2-1031-5c3b-942d-069e218dd818" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7e7acc82-e6c4-5dec-befb-675ccec0903d" "answer-0c7c5dd4-985b-5405-bb39-51253042d52c" "answer-919301d7-68f2-5782-915a-8f4d378d4451" "answer-cffa05d2-1031-5c3b-942d-069e218dd818"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e7acc82-e6c4-5dec-befb-675ccec0903d" :answer/id #uuid "7e7acc82-e6c4-5dec-befb-675ccec0903d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c7c5dd4-985b-5405-bb39-51253042d52c" :answer/id #uuid "0c7c5dd4-985b-5405-bb39-51253042d52c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-919301d7-68f2-5782-915a-8f4d378d4451" :answer/id #uuid "919301d7-68f2-5782-915a-8f4d378d4451" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cffa05d2-1031-5c3b-942d-069e218dd818" :answer/id #uuid "cffa05d2-1031-5c3b-942d-069e218dd818" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2b80139d-bc1e-5645-8de4-30d2db8afdf1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2b80139d-bc1e-5645-8de4-30d2db8afdf1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-b39d43c4-a1f9-54c2-8f94-0bfd592ebd10" :answer-field/choices ["answer-14bc55e1-31d7-5798-9f51-9e810ae5b145" "answer-b39d43c4-a1f9-54c2-8f94-0bfd592ebd10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14bc55e1-31d7-5798-9f51-9e810ae5b145" :answer/id #uuid "14bc55e1-31d7-5798-9f51-9e810ae5b145" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b39d43c4-a1f9-54c2-8f94-0bfd592ebd10" :answer/id #uuid "b39d43c4-a1f9-54c2-8f94-0bfd592ebd10" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4ad00cd6-77d9-5b4b-9995-246f45c8c4b3" :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad00cd6-77d9-5b4b-9995-246f45c8c4b3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-6a24f1ef-649e-5645-995e-2f802b1f4380" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6a24f1ef-649e-5645-995e-2f802b1f4380" "answer-c09b78a5-7042-5294-99d6-32a590ecb504"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a24f1ef-649e-5645-995e-2f802b1f4380" :answer/id #uuid "6a24f1ef-649e-5645-995e-2f802b1f4380" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c09b78a5-7042-5294-99d6-32a590ecb504" :answer/id #uuid "c09b78a5-7042-5294-99d6-32a590ecb504" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-80de76c1-383f-57a7-b028-3b3844fe8b00" :answer-field/id #uuid "80de76c1-383f-57a7-b028-3b3844fe8b00" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-2e978d2d-0d3b-58eb-a366-69213e6f3246" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2e978d2d-0d3b-58eb-a366-69213e6f3246" "answer-116aa627-74f3-55db-9c3e-276499470af8" "answer-88101903-0398-5bd1-af51-4e947d7e70ca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e978d2d-0d3b-58eb-a366-69213e6f3246" :answer/id #uuid "2e978d2d-0d3b-58eb-a366-69213e6f3246" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-116aa627-74f3-55db-9c3e-276499470af8" :answer/id #uuid "116aa627-74f3-55db-9c3e-276499470af8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88101903-0398-5bd1-af51-4e947d7e70ca" :answer/id #uuid "88101903-0398-5bd1-af51-4e947d7e70ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308485 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-6,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$\n$y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-2}} is {{field-3}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307664 :answer-field/correct 17592186306830 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307896 :answer-field/correct 17592186307321 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307744 :answer-field/correct 17592186305344 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168021 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we'll study *phase portraits* of linear systems\n![](images/6f/6f6a92e2b8fb89f833268a5526876cb3af5215db0193a2aaaad8aac5790752ea.svg)\nin the case where the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ has two *real, distinct* eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ with corresponding eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2.$\n\nIf $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c\\,e^{\\lambda t}\\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\ngives us a solution that represents a line through the origin parallel to $\\mathbf{v}.$ So, when $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ are real and distinct, we always have two special straight-line solutions:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1 e^{\\lambda_1 t}\\mathbf{v}_1\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_2 e^{\\lambda_2 t}\\mathbf{v}_2\n$$\n\nAlong the line parallel to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}$:\n\n- If $\\lambda<0,$ then $e^{\\lambda t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to\\infty,$ so trajectories move *toward* the origin (*stable line*).\n\n- If $\\lambda>0,$ then $e^{\\lambda t}\\to\\infty$ as $t\\to\\infty,$ so trajectories move *away* from the origin (*unstable line*).\n\nUsually, to sketch the phase portrait, we proceed as follows:\n\n1\\. Draw the two eigenvector lines through the origin (one line parallel to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and another parallel to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_2$).\n\n2\\. Add arrows on each eigenvector line:\n\n  - toward the origin if its eigenvalue is negative, or\n\n  - away from the origin if its eigenvalue is positive.\n\n3\\. Add a few curved trajectories that are generally \"pulled\" toward the stable direction(s) and \"pushed\" along unstable direction(s).\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168000 :tutorial/content "Sketch the phase portrait of the system of linear differential equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ngiven that the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=-4$ and $\\lambda_2=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ![](images/38/38145b0a5b121bacb02c6099d44a67e4dc69f8bd56886520705f72bf8914c1be.svg) and ![](images/a7/a703180de3d09425c5851fb61ba1252f9678bae0d0d46f09841c558eddce2f53.svg) respectively.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the system is\n![](images/0f/0f26128d359ffa6aa2f5e970532cb45d17172035b8265232d79fb0f885727c9d.svg)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/05/05929fabda1451bdfef2600375b1c2958170cad989a50dadd35ad4039fc5d9af.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny =-\\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/08/0838cdd1e0a3d3603c24565276255fdb6d1eb2adf410f085058e954c5b153338.svg)\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/14/1459d79913dec89577405a506b8d4adbbf4cb05abb51948eedae76d8f71544b8.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/a3/a3350d2791e5dbb9662affd4dd64d36edae7a32198e0a3da5c6567a211ce3395.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/04/04d96c354e7d03cf0b88d0ee29bc7097b552b6959cf76c88012af8758794e0a9.svg)\nlie on that line.\n\nSince $e^{2t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/fd/fd53399b3d0c5ec40adcf91aaae4aca5ce55a9a7b574e9223fde615afc3b769f.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/bf/bf0c26af74326dd108d42eec1493b0ddf64cfdf0744c91b55e732b80b7d0835c.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168007 :tutorial/content "Let's sketch the phase portrait of the system of linear differential equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ngiven that the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ![](images/d7/d71caa68afca9754733d4e2a297aa5797a0557d5f54e31f27b4a594179502739.svg) and ![](images/36/36065bd1dab3ced89501cf11b7891095c0e7b42e065a3bbdfa4bc5bd8a365368.svg) respectively.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the system is\n![](images/95/95bb67ded561bb289e4fd81ddddcec6eec2685201a8e2e0fca42bbcecbccf3d2.svg)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/5b/5bca9f7fd018a8177f885b099b73a471a70568e799564c94061d78ce23b2c9a6.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= 2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/1c/1c0def9e97c023fd4e71c146dae04d93cc0f6fed126a5c650f042f0500fda7d4.svg)\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{5t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c7/c7c824e97d40d2f982525040d5f0ba697c34c40ff51700ec3952d2909d566e21.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/00/00bf863ed71e61386b9f755abfe7152e36474d68404204e0f1c0633828d3735c.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/32/3246939f411072280c878c2b055f329d69ff48568a9a8ad8301c0a0b04f58ddd.svg)\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{2t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c3/c3030613b03a1afabd964d9494bf2359bd6ce5f021c731feda9607a81aaf5df6.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n\n$$\n0 < \\lambda_2 < \\lambda_1\n$$\n\nthus, $\\lambda_1 = 5$ is the dominant eigenvalue.\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is $\\lambda_1=5,$ solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/43/43c6294fa7f68b21422eee71d35edd5f7880b3b3c9e23a2a8078090416cded05.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168022 :tutorial/content "Finally, let’s summarize what we’ve learned so far.\n\nAgain, we are considering a linear system\n![](images/86/869f75b82137ebde181b22be0d6734196363444e9c53cb9e77deafc975931dd9.svg)\nin the case where the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ has two *real, distinct* eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ with corresponding eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2.$\n\nRecall that if $\\lambda_1, \\lambda_2 \\neq 0,$ the system has a single equilibrium point at the origin $(0,0).$\nTo classify this equilibrium, we look at the signs of $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2.$\n\nFirst, we describe the straight-line solutions:\n\n- Along the line parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_1,$ solutions have the form $\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1e^{\\lambda_1 t}\\mathbf{v}_1$ where $c_1 \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- If $\\lambda_1<0,$ it's a *stable* line (direction).\n\n- If $\\lambda_1>0,$ it's an *unstable* line (direction).\n\n- Along the line parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_2,$ solutions have the form $\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_2e^{\\lambda_2 t}\\mathbf{v}_2$ where $c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- If $\\lambda_2<0,$ it's a *stable* line (direction).\n\n- If $\\lambda_2>0,$ it's an *unstable* line (direction).\n\nAs for the classification of the equilibrium at the origin, we get the following:\n\n- *Unstable node (source)* if $\\lambda_1>0$ and $\\lambda_2>0.$\n\n- *Stable node (sink)* if $\\lambda_1<0$ and $\\lambda_2<0.$ In fact, it's asymptotically stable, as the trajectories converge to the origin.\n\n- *Saddle point* if $\\lambda_1\\lambda_2<0$ (one positive, one negative).\n\nLet's apply this classification to concrete examples." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167010 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
